    <!-- Main Content -->
    <main>
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <section class="hero">
            <h2>Database Management Systems & C Programming Guide</h2>
        </section>

        <!-- DBMS Section -->
        <section id="dbms-section" class="panel">
            <h2 id="dbms-intro">Database Management System</h2>
            
            <h3>What is DBMS?</h3>
            <p>A Database Management System (DBMS) is software that interacts with users, applications, and the database itself to capture and analyze data.</p>
            
            <h3 id="dbms-models">DBMS Models</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Model</th>
                        <th>Advantages</th>
                        <th>Disadvantages</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Hierarchical</td>
                        <td>Simple structure, fast access</td>
                        <td>Rigid structure, complex to modify</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Relational</td>
                        <td>Simple and flexible, supports SQL</td>
                        <td>Performance issues with complex queries</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>NoSQL</td>
                        <td>Handles unstructured data, scales well</td>
                        <td>Limited query capabilities</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 id="relational-db">Relational Database</h3>
            <p>A relational database organizes data into tables (relations) with rows and columns, where relationships between tables are established using keys.</p>
            
            <h3 id="normalization">Normalization Process</h3>
            <p>Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.</p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>1NF</strong>: Eliminate repeating groups</li>
                <li><strong>2NF</strong>: Remove partial dependencies</li>
                <li><strong>3NF</strong>: Remove transitive dependencies</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 id="sql-basics">SQL Basics</h3>
            <p>SQL (Structured Query Language) is a standard language for managing and manipulating relational databases.</p>
            <h4>Example SQL Commands:</h4>
            <pre><code>CREATE TABLE pirates (
    pirate_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    bounty BIGINT,
    crew VARCHAR(50)
);

INSERT INTO pirates VALUES 
(1, 'Monkey D. Luffy', 3000000000, 'Straw Hats'),
(2, 'Roronoa Zoro', 1111000000, 'Straw Hats');

SELECT * FROM pirates WHERE bounty > 1000000000;
</code></pre>
        </section>

        <!-- C Programming Section -->
        <section id="c-section" class="panel">
            <h2>C Programming Concepts</h2>
            
            <h3 id="functions">Functions in C</h3>
            <p>A function is a self-contained block of code that performs a specific task.</p>
            
            <h4>Function Example:</h4>
            <pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

// Function declaration
int calculateBounty(int years);

int main() {
    int luffyBounty = calculateBounty(3);
    printf("Luffy's current bounty: %d berries\n", luffyBounty);
    return 0;
}

// Function definition
int calculateBounty(int years) {
    if(years == 0) {
        return 30000000; // initial bounty
    }
    else {
        return 2 * calculateBounty(years - 1);
    }
}</code></pre>

            <h3 id="recursion">Recursive Functions</h3>
            <p>A recursive function is one that calls itself directly or indirectly.</p>
            
            <h4>Recursion Example:</h4>
            <pre><code>void devilFruitPowers(int level) {
    if(level <= 0) {
        printf("No power awakened.\n");
        return;
    }
    printf("Awakening level %d power!\n", level);
    devilFruitPowers(level - 1);
}</code></pre>
        </section>

        <!-- Examples Section -->
        <section id="examples" class="panel">
            <h2>Code Examples</h2>
            
            <h3>Storage Classes in C</h3>
            <pre><code>// Automatic storage class
void devilFruit() {
    auto int power = 1; // automatic variable
}

// Static storage class
void countHakiUses() {
    static int count = 0; // retains value between calls
    count++;
    printf("Haki used %d times\n", count);
}</code></pre>

            <h3>SQL Table Creation</h3>
            <pre><code>CREATE TABLE devil_fruits (
    fruit_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    type VARCHAR(30),
    current_user VARCHAR(50),
    FOREIGN KEY (current_user) REFERENCES pirates(name)
);</code></pre>
        </section>
    </main>

    <script>
        // Toggle sidebar
        document.getElementById('menuButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
            document.getElementById('sidebar').classList.toggle('active');
        });

        // Close sidebar when clicking outside
        document.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
            const sidebar = document.getElementById('sidebar');
            const menuButton = document.getElementById('menuButton');
            
            if (sidebar.classList.contains('active') && 
                !sidebar.contains(event.target) && 
                event.target !== menuButton) {
                sidebar.classList.remove('active');
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
